## Design

**1. DB-level audit table + trigger, not app-level logging.**
Triggers fire on every `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` regardless of which code path (or future developer, or `psql` session) issued it — an app-layer `try/log` wrapper only catches writes that go through code you remembered to instrument.

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_pk      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL,           -- INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE
  actor_id    uuid,                    -- nullable: system/migration writes
  old_row     jsonb,
  new_row     jsonb,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_pk, action, actor_id, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    current_setting('app.actor_id', true)::uuid,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit   AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

**2. Get "who" to the trigger via a session-local setting, set in `write()`.**
`req.user.id` is already resolved for every request by `sessionUser` (`auth.js:11`). Change `write()`'s signature to take an actor id and issue `SET LOCAL app.actor_id` right after `BEGIN`, before the real statement — no per-route changes needed beyond passing `req.user.id` in:

```js
async function write(text, params, actorId) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actorId || null]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } ...
}
```
`SET LOCAL`/`set_config(..., true)` is transaction-scoped, so it can't leak across pooled connections between requests.

**3. Make the audit trail tamper-resistant from the app itself.**
Grant the app's DB role `INSERT`-only on nothing at all — it should never write `audit_log` directly, only the trigger (owned by a different role) does. Revoke `UPDATE`/`DELETE` on `audit_log` from every role, including admins if possible; treat it as append-only. For real tamper-evidence, periodically ship it to WORM storage (S3 Object Lock) or a separate DB the app role can't reach.

